      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The argument that UBI is simpler ignores the massive, hidden costs of a truly universal payout, which would necessitate crippling tax increases or runaway national debt. Targeted welfare, when properly streamlined, allows for the strategic redirection of funds toward productive investments like job retraining and healthcare access, which are far more stabilizing than simply handing out unconditional cash. Furthermore, the \"benefits cliff\" issue is a solvable policy design problem, not an inherent flaw in the concept of targeted support.",
      "source": "target",
